  "notes": "DC a cappella-forward quartet: doo-wop harmony against a minimal programmed bed, 1992–1995. The research gives \"61–72 half-time (backbeat on 3)\", encoded as the felt band with `halfTime` true and `halftime_3`. The three drum deltas follow the entry's signature claim — \"the signature is drums *absent* for whole sections; when present, kick on 1, snare on 3, no hats\": a 0.35 `beatSkipProb` with one or two kicks a bar, a hat lane at 0.01 fill density on an 8th base with the open hat off, and a two-lane snap/rim bed at −12 dB. The identifying parameter is not the kit at all but the harmony: \"four-part close harmony as the entire arrangement — the group's identity is that the chord progression is sung, not played… barbershop-adjacent voicings with the melody in the second voice; ii–V–I turnarounds\". So the families are functional major turnarounds rather than modal vamps, `voicing.style` is close, `chordToneBias` is 0.92 — the highest in this batch — and the countermelody carries an unusually high density with `octave_echo` leading, because the counter here is another sung voice rather than an instrument. The bass is \"bass voice (human) covers the low register on the a cappella passages; synth bass on roots elsewhere\", so it is a bass part with root-fifth cells and no `bass808`. ⚠ The research marks the key claim `[LOW-CONF]` — \"major-mode with heavy 7th/9th colour\" — and that is what the extension spread encodes, without naming tonics the research does not name. ⚠ Every velocity, density and register number here is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"major-mode with heavy 7th/9th colour [LOW-CONF]\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line.",
